        <section id="why-code-is-reviewed"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Why code is reviewed</h2>
              <p>Carrying out code reviews provide a range of benefits, scroll through the slides below to learn about these.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Consistent design and implementation
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>In the previous session we’ve already considered the importance of keeping code simple (KISS) and standardised. Reviewing code ensures that all developers are maintaining this method of working and a consistent style is adopted throughout the project. This ensures longevity of the programme and changes that are needed can be carried out by new staff years later.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p1_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Minimising mistakes
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Anyone can make mistakes, and this is more likely if you’re working under pressure and are spending too long working on the same code. Finding your own mistakes can be difficult and a colleague is more likely to pick out small errors quite quickly. This is not only more efficient, but can also incorporate benefits of the wider code review e.g. learning new techniques.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p1_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Quality
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Individuals involved in a project may interpret some elements of code differently. The review process can ensure that all developers keep to the agreed standard and don’t become lost or get carried away with their own ideas, which can happen if working in isolation. Having someone check your code to ensure that nothing’s been overlooked can save a lot of time further down the line and assist with the final QA process.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p1_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Performance
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The code review can help increase the skills of inexperienced developers. It’s a chance for them to learn from more experienced colleagues how they might have applied their code differently with more optimized techniques.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p1_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          New technologies
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Sharing the code review between colleagues allows everyone to understand different techniques and develop their skill set. It allows new technologies to be shared in an equal environment where newer coders may teach more experienced ones the latest trends. It also allows more experienced developers can help newer ones understand the importance of tried and tested techniques.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p1_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
